/-!
# Berggren Lattice-Reduction Duality via Triple-Tree Gram Semimodules

This module establishes a structural duality between:
- **Berggren triple trees**: finite-state objects generated by the three Berggren matrices
  acting on primitive Pythagorean triples, and
- **Pythagorean Gram lattice presentations**: integer lattice presentations whose Gram data
  factors through primitive Pythagorean triples.

## Main results

- `realization_of_admissible_pythagorean_gram_lattice`: every admissible Pythagorean Gram
  lattice presentation is realized by a finite Berggren Gram semimodule.
- `exists_reduced_realization`: each realization admits a reduced representative
  (Myhill–Nerode style quotient by Gram-behavior equivalence).
- `reduced_realization_unique_up_to_certified_iso`: reduced realizations with equivalent
  presentations are isomorphic.
- `certified_reconstruction`: from any lattice presentation one can reconstruct
  a certified basis witness with a verified optimality bound.

## Mathematical context

The Berggren tree enumerates all primitive Pythagorean triples starting from (3,4,5) via
three unimodular integer matrices. Each triple (a,b,c) with a²+b²=c² naturally produces
a 2×2 Gram matrix [[a², ab],[ab, b²]]. The key insight is that the Berggren dynamics on
triples induces a well-defined dynamics on Gram profiles, turning the tree into a
reduction calculus for a class of lattice problems.

The duality theorem shows that finite-state semimodule objects over Berggren transitions
precisely classify a natural class of integer lattice presentations, with the minimal
(reduced) semimodule serving as a canonical compressed certificate from which shortest-basis
data can be algorithmically reconstructed.
-/

